<section vertical-align="center">
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item>
      <h2>Hospitality onstage; choreography backstage</h2>
      <labels>
        <label variant="outline">Illustrative operating model</label>
        <label variant="solid">2 sessions per day</label>
        <label variant="solid">8 places per session</label>
        <label variant="solid">15-minute room turn</label>
      </labels>
      <p>The guest sees a calm hour. The property runs a controlled sequence of inventory, room standards, qualified delivery, handoffs, and records.</p>
      <grid recipe="arrows" vertical-sizing="hug">
        <grid-item colspan="12">
          <h4>Offer</h4>
          <p font-size="sm">Reservations or front desk present the ritual before arrival, at check-in, or in stay. One inventory controls availability.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="12">
          <h4>Prepare</h4>
          <p font-size="sm">The hotel host checks the accessible route, water, linens, seating, temperature, and approved materials.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="12">
          <h4>Deliver</h4>
          <p font-size="sm">The practitioner owns the practice and safety boundaries; the host owns the welcome and handoff.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="12">
          <h4>Reset</h4>
          <p font-size="sm">The room returns to a documented standard before the next session.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item colspan="12">
          <h4>Learn</h4>
          <p font-size="sm">Record attendance, anonymous feedback, staff handling time, and direct costs—without unnecessary health information.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>